l projection matrix"] [17592186134179 :question/problem "$a = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}p_{11} & p_{12} & p_{13} \\\\ p_{21} & p 22 & p_{23} \\\\ p 31 & p_{32} & p_{33}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that the vector $a$ above spans $S^{⊥}$, and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of $R^{3}$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of $p_{22} + p_{31}$?"] [17592186133520 :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the subspace spanned by $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186133520 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix\n\n$$\nP_S = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\n$$\n\nmaps any vector $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A$.\n\nIn our case, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{S} &= A(A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix})^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (14)^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133599 :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the plane whose parametric equation is $\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v} + t\\mathbf{w}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186133599 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix\n\n$$\nP_S = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\n$$\n\nmaps any vector $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A$.\n\nNotice that the plane\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v}+t\\mathbf{w}\n$$\n\nis actually the subspace $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w}\\}$.\nHence, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{S} &= A(A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133521 :question/problem "Which of the following are orthogonal projection matrices?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186133521 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if $Q^2=Q$ and $Q^T=Q$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* an orthogonal projection matrix. We have\n$A^T = A$\nbut\n$A^{2}|= A \\cdot A; = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10\\end{bmatrix}; \\ne A$.\n- $B$ is *not* an orthogonal projection matrix. We have\n$B^{T} \\ne B$.\n- $C$ is an orthogonal projection matrix. Indeed,\n$C^T = C$\nand\n$C^{2}|= C \\cdot C; = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}; = \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}20 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}; = C$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\""] [17592186133522 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ above spans $S^\\perp$, and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of $p_{13} + p_{32}$?"] [17592186133522 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the orthogonal projection matrix onto\n\n$$\nS^\\perp = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the orthogonal projection matrix\n\n$$\nP_{S^\\perp} = \\mathbf{a} (\\mathbf{a}^T\\!\\mathbf{a})^{-1} \\mathbf{a}^T\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{S^{⊥}} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\nP_S + P_{S^\\perp} = I\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{S} &= I - P_{S^{⊥}} \\\\\n&= I - \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\np_{13} + p_{32} = (-2) + (-2) = -4\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186131511 175921861